{"call_id":"call_MluXvkqjSI73u6m37ROUVVQx","event_id":"ctco_01a09032-176e-7403-9f89-4da5b1c9635e","output":[{"text":"Script completed\nWall time 1.5 seconds\nOutput:\n","type":"input_text"},{"text":"{\"i\":0,\"status\":\"fulfilled\",\"value\":\"Materialization · git-meta (https://git-meta.com/spec/exchange-format/materialization.html)\\nciteturn0view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last week; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\\\"ref_id\\\":\\\"https://git-meta.com/spec/exchange-format/materialization.html\\\",\\\"lineno\\\":null}); Total lines: 127\\nL0: cite0†git-meta L1: \\nL2: Git metadata specification and project docs\\nL3: \\nL4: cite0†Overview L5: \\nL6: ## Exchange format\\nL7: \\nL8: cite1†Targets and keys cite2†Serialization cite3†Materialization L9: \\nL10: ## Value types\\nL11: \\nL12: cite4†Strings cite5†Lists cite6†Sets L13: \\nL14: ## Implementation\\nL15: \\nL16: cite7†Implementation Overview cite8†Rust Library API cite9†Local storage cite10†CLI surface cite11†Output and query semantics cite12†Auto-pruning cite13†Serialize filters L17: ## Other Considerations\\nL18: \\nL19: cite14†Standard keys cite15†Remote Management cite16†Syncing with normal Git cite17†Workflow L20: \\nL21: ☀️ 🌙\\nL22: \\nL23: ◧\\nL24: \\nL25: Generated from cite18†`spec/*.md`†github.com L26: \\nL27: # Materialization\\nL28: \\nL29: cite19†{ } markdown†raw.githubusercontent.com L30: \\nL31: This document describes the rules for turning a serialized metadata tree back into local current state.\\nL32: ## Materialize responsibilities\\nL33: \\nL34: When materializing a remote metadata head, the client must:\\nL35: \\nL36:   1. merge that head in memory into local serialized metadata\\nL37:   2. resolve merge conflicts according to value-type semantics\\nL38:   3. update the local SQLite database with newly visible values and tombstones\\nL39:   4. record when materialization succeeded\\nL40: \\nL41: Per-type conflict rules are defined in:\\nL42: \\nL43:   * cite4†Strings L44:   * cite5†Lists L45:   * cite6†Sets L46: \\nL47: ## High-level scenarios\\nL48: ### 1. Initial sync\\nL49: \\nL50: If the local system has no metadata yet:\\nL51: \\nL52:   * walk the tree at the incoming metadata head\\nL53:   * materialize all visible values into SQLite\\nL54:   * record that metadata commit as materialized\\nL55: \\nL56: No metadata history walk is required beyond the current tree.\\nL57: ### 2. Multiple start points\\nL58: \\nL59: If local metadata exists and someone else already pushed an independently initialized metadata history:\\nL60: \\nL61:   * serialize local shareable state\\nL62:   * perform a baseless two-way merge with the remote tree\\nL63:   * for overlapping keys, remote wins\\nL64:   * write a new metadata commit with the remote head as parent\\nL65:   * retry if the remote advanced before push completed\\nL66: \\nL67: This allows eventual convergence while keeping history linear.\\nL68: ### 3. Fast-forward update\\nL69: \\nL70: If the incoming metadata ref is a fast-forward from the last materialized point:\\nL71: \\nL72:   * diff the old materialized tree against the new tree\\nL73:   * apply only changed key/values to SQLite\\nL74:   * add or update visible values and tombstones\\nL75: ### 4. Both sides mutated data\\nL76: \\nL77: If local data changed and remote data also changed:\\nL78: \\nL79:   * serialize local current shareable state\\nL80:   * merge the remote tree into it\\nL81:   * resolve conflicts according to per-type semantics\\nL82:   * write a new metadata commit from the merged tree\\nL83: \\nL84: On push, if it is again not a fast-forward, do this sequence repeatedly against newer remote heads until a fast-forward push succeeds. Only one new commit locally needs to be made.\\nL85: ## No common ancestor m